(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["evals",{"l:evals/eval-driven-development-and-the-improvement-loop":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>eval\u003c/b> is a test for an AI system: an input, the system's output, and grading logic that says pass or fail (or gives a score). Evals turn \"it feels better\" into a number, so every prompt, model or retrieval change becomes an experiment with a result. Teams that improve fastest spend most of their time looking at data and writing evals, not tweaking prompts by feel.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why evals are the core skill\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ordinary software has a specification: a function either returns the right value or it does not. An AI system has no such contract. The same input can give different outputs, a one-line prompt change can break cases you never looked at, and a provider can update a model under you. Without evals, development turns into whack-a-mole: a user complains, you tweak the prompt, the complaint goes away, and three other behaviours quietly get worse.\u003c/p>\u003cp>With evals you get four things:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Regression protection.\u003c/b> Every change runs against the cases that used to work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fast model upgrades.\u003c/b> Anthropic notes that teams with evals can assess a new model and upgrade in days, while teams without them face weeks of manual testing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A shared definition of done.\u003c/b> Writing a test case forces product and engineering to agree on what a good answer is.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Baselines for free.\u003c/b> Once the suite exists, you also track latency, tokens and cost per task on a fixed set of inputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar, who have helped dozens of companies build evals, report spending 60 to 80% of development time on error analysis and evaluation. That is not overhead: it is where the improvements come from.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Vocabulary\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Case (task)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One input with success criteria\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trial\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One run of the system on a case; outputs vary, so you may run several\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grader (evaluator)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Logic that scores an output: code, an LLM judge or a human\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trace (transcript)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The full record of a run: prompts, retrieved text, tool calls, outputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Outcome\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The final state of the world, such as whether a ticket really exists\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Suite\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A collection of cases that share a purpose\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The improvement loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Look at traces\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Name and count failures\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Write evals for the top failures\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Change prompt, context, model or code\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Re-run evals\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Ship and monitor\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The loop has three phases, often called \u003cb>analyse, measure, improve\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Analyse.\u003c/b> Read real traces and write down what goes wrong (the next lesson). The output is a short list of failure modes with counts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure.\u003c/b> Build evaluators for the failure modes that matter: code checks where a rule can decide, LLM judges where judgement is needed. Run them on a dataset to get a baseline.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Improve.\u003c/b> Change one thing, re-run, compare with the baseline. Ship if it helps, then watch production, which feeds new traces into the next round of analysis.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Three levels of evaluation\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>When it runs\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1. Unit tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code assertions on outputs: format, citations, forbidden content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every change, in CI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds, fractions of a cent\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2. Model and human eval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LLM judges and people reading traces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nightly, per release, weekly review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minutes, a few dollars\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3. A/B testing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Real users split between versions, business metrics compared\u003c/td>\u003ctd>After launch, for big changes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Days, needs traffic\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Most of your suite should be Level 1, because it is cheap and deterministic. Level 2 covers the qualities only judgement can assess. Level 3 is the final word but is slow and needs traffic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Capability evals and regression evals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>capability eval\u003c/b> asks \"what can the system do?\" It should start with a low pass rate and give the team a hill to climb. A \u003cb>regression eval\u003c/b> asks \"does it still do everything it used to?\" and should sit near 100%; any drop means something broke. As capability cases become reliably solved, they graduate into the regression suite.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Should evals come before the feature?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\"Eval-driven development\" means writing the eval before the behaviour exists, like test-driven development. Anthropic recommends it for planned capabilities: a capability eval that starts at 0% makes a bet visible, and when a new model ships you see at once whether it pays off. Husain and Shankar push back on doing this for everything: LLMs fail in ways you cannot predict, so most evaluators should be written for errors you \u003ci>discover\u003c/i> in traces, not errors you \u003ci>imagine\u003c/i>. The exception is a hard requirement known up front, such as \"never mention competitors\", which you can test from day one. In practice, teams do both: specify known requirements as evals early, and let error analysis drive the rest.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start small. Anthropic suggests 20 to 50 tasks drawn from real failures; early changes have large effects, so small sets detect them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep evals in the repository next to the code, owned like unit tests, with domain experts allowed to add cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Never take a score at face value until someone has read the failing transcripts. Anthropic describes a model scoring 42% on the CORE-Bench benchmark until researchers found grading bugs (for example, rejecting \"96.12\" when the expected value was 96.124991...) and other task issues; after fixes the score was 95%. Graders have bugs too.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between a capability eval and a regression eval?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A capability eval measures what the system can do and starts with a low pass rate to give a target to improve towards. A regression eval checks that behaviour which used to work still works, so it should pass at nearly 100% and any drop signals a break. Capability cases that become reliably solved move into the regression suite.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name the three phases of the improvement loop and what each one produces.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Analyse produces a list of named failure modes with counts, from reading traces. Measure produces evaluators (code checks and judges) plus a dataset and a baseline score. Improve produces a change and new scores compared with the baseline; production traces from the shipped change feed the next round of analysis.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is it reasonable to write an evaluator before you have seen any failures?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the requirement is a hard, known constraint whose success is unambiguous, such as \"never mention competitors\", \"always return valid JSON\" or a planned capability you are betting on. For everything else, write evaluators for failures you find in traces, because LLM failure modes are hard to predict.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should most of an eval suite be Level 1 code assertions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They are deterministic, fast and almost free, so they can run on every change in CI without noise or cost. Judges and humans are slower, cost money and need validation, so they are reserved for qualities that a rule cannot decide.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:error-analysis-and-open-coding-of-failures":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Error analysis\u003c/b> means reading real traces, writing a short note on what went wrong in each one (\u003cb>open coding\u003c/b>), grouping the notes into a handful of failure modes (\u003cb>axial coding\u003c/b>) and counting them. The counts tell you what to fix first and which evals to write. It is the highest-return activity in AI engineering, because no generic metric can tell you how \u003ci>your\u003c/i> system fails.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why start here\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Teams new to evals often begin with a list of metrics they found online: hallucination, toxicity, helpfulness, coherence. Those names sound right but rarely match the actual problems. A docs assistant might never be toxic yet constantly answer for the wrong software version. Error analysis replaces guessing with observation. The terms come from qualitative research, where they describe how researchers find themes in interview notes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Step 1: gather traces\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>trace\u003c/b> is the complete record of one interaction: the user input, the prompt as actually sent, retrieved documents, tool calls and results, and the final output. Gather a pool of about 100 diverse traces and plan to annotate at least the first 30 to 50 yourself.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>If you have production traffic, sample randomly and add a few traces from each important feature, plus some with negative feedback.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If you have no users yet, generate realistic inputs (the next lesson shows how) and run them through the system.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a view that shows everything in one screen: a notebook, a spreadsheet or a 50-line custom viewer. Husain argues that a simple data viewer is the most important tool an AI team can build.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Step 2: open coding\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read each trace end to end and write one or two sentences about what went wrong, plus a pass or fail. Be concrete: \"Answered for Kubernetes 1.24 although user said 1.30\" beats \"wrong answer\". Note the \u003cb>first\u003c/b> failure in the trace, because upstream errors cause downstream ones: if retrieval missed the right page, the hallucinated answer that follows is a symptom, not a separate problem.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Trace\u003c/th>\u003cth>Pass?\u003c/th>\u003cth>Note\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>t07\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieved the 1.24 upgrade page; user asked about 1.30\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>t08\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Correct, cited two pages\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>t09\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Says \u003ccode>--force\u003c/code> flag exists; no retrieved doc mentions it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>t10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Created a ticket without asking for the cluster name\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>t11\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Correct steps, but no citation at all\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not invent categories yet. Write what you see.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Step 3: axial coding\u003c/h4>\u003cp>When you have 30 to 50 notes, group them into 4 to 8 \u003cb>failure modes\u003c/b> that are specific and actionable. You can paste the notes into an LLM and ask it to propose groupings, but you decide the final names. Then count.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Example: 50 Atlas traces, 26 pass and 24 fail.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Failure mode (first failure only)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Count\u003c/th>\u003cth>Share of traces\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unsupported claim: detail not in any retrieved doc\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval missed the relevant page\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>12%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ignored a constraint in the question (version, OS)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Missing or wrong citation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool called with missing or wrong arguments\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Answer buried in a long preamble\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A \u003cb>pivot table\u003c/b> (spreadsheet or pandas) of failure mode against feature or user type often reveals more: perhaps every version error comes from questions about upgrades.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Step 4: prioritise and act\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rank failure modes by frequency, severity and ease of fixing. Then decide per mode:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Just fix it.\u003c/b> Many failures come from an instruction you never wrote (\"always ask for the cluster name before creating a ticket\"). Fix the prompt and move on; not every failure needs an evaluator.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write an evaluator\u003c/b> for modes that persist after a fix or matter a lot: a code check if a rule can detect it (missing citation), an LLM judge if it needs judgement (unsupported claim).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Add the failing traces to your dataset\u003c/b> as regression cases.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Keep reading until new traces stop revealing new failure modes. Qualitative researchers call this \u003cb>theoretical saturation\u003c/b>. Then repeat the whole exercise after big changes and on a regular schedule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scoring on a 1 to 5 scale.\u003c/b> Use pass or fail plus a note. Scales hide indecision in the middle values and annotators disagree about what a 3 means.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Delegating to people without domain knowledge.\u003c/b> Use one domain expert as the final arbiter, sometimes called a \"benevolent dictator\". If you are building alone, that is you.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expecting fixed criteria.\u003c/b> Shankar and colleagues named the effect \u003cb>criteria drift\u003c/b>: you need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs changes your criteria. Expect your failure-mode list to change during the first rounds; that is the process working.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Letting an LLM do the whole thing.\u003c/b> It can suggest groupings and flag likely failures, but it does not know what your users need. You must read the traces.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you read 50 traces, label failure modes and count them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Collect 50 diverse traces (random production samples plus some from key features and negative feedback, or synthetic inputs run through the system). Read each end to end and write a one or two sentence note on the first thing that went wrong, with pass or fail (open coding). Group the notes into 4 to 8 specific failure modes, optionally with an LLM proposing groupings that you review (axial coding). Assign each failing trace to one mode, count with a spreadsheet or pandas, compute each mode's share of traces, and rank by frequency and severity to decide what to fix or evaluate first.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why record only the first failure in each trace?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Errors cascade: a retrieval miss often causes a hallucinated answer and a missing citation. Counting the root failure points you at the cause rather than inflating the count of downstream symptoms.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is theoretical saturation and how does it tell you when to stop?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the point at which reading more traces stops revealing new failure modes or changing the existing ones. When the last 10 to 20 traces only add counts to known categories, you have enough to act.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is criteria drift?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The finding by Shankar and colleagues that people need criteria to grade outputs, yet grading outputs changes their criteria. Definitions of failure modes shift as you read more data, so expect to revise them and to re-label early traces.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:building-eval-datasets":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An eval dataset is a versioned file of test cases, each an input plus a description of what \"good\" means for it: a reference answer, facts that must or must not appear, or grading notes. Good datasets mix sampled real traffic, every failure you have confirmed and targeted synthetic cases for rare situations, and they test what the system should \u003ci>not\u003c/i> do as well as what it should.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What a case looks like\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store cases as JSONL (one JSON object per line) in your repository or in your eval platform. A case for Atlas, the docs assistant you build in this guide:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"id\": \"kb-0042\",\n \"input\": \"How do I rotate the API key for the staging cluster without downtime?\",\n \"expected\": {\n   \"must_include\": [\"create the new key before revoking the old one\"],\n   \"must_not_include\": [\"--force\"],\n   \"should_call_tool\": null,\n   \"grading_notes\": \"Must cite the key-rotation page. Must not claim rotation is automatic.\"\n },\n \"tags\": [\"feature:security\", \"scenario:procedure\", \"source:prod\"],\n \"source\": \"trace-8f2c41\",\n \"split\": \"dev\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reference answer or grading notes.\u003c/b> For questions with one right answer, store it. For open-ended answers, store the facts a good answer must contain and the mistakes it must avoid. Notes are easier to write and easier for graders to apply than a full model answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tags\u003c/b> let you slice results: a 2-point overall drop might hide a 15-point drop on upgrade questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Source\u003c/b> records provenance, so you can trace a case back to the original conversation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Where cases come from\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical share\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sampled production traces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about half\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Matches the real distribution of requests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Confirmed failures (error analysis, bug reports, thumbs-down)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a quarter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regression protection for things that actually broke\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Targeted synthetic cases\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 to 20%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rare scenarios, new features, adversarial inputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hand-written edge cases\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a few\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Known hard constraints and policies\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Synthetic data done well\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Asking a model to \"give me 50 test questions\" produces generic, repetitive queries. Instead, define \u003cb>dimensions\u003c/b> along which inputs vary, enumerate combinations, then turn each combination into a natural query.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Dimension\u003c/th>\u003cth>Values for Atlas\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Feature\u003c/td>\u003ctd>install, upgrade, security, billing, troubleshooting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scenario\u003c/td>\u003ctd>clear question, ambiguous, no answer in docs, wrong assumption, multi-step\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Persona\u003c/td>\u003ctd>new user, expert, non-native speaker, in a hurry\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import itertools, random, json\n\nfeatures = [\"install\", \"upgrade\", \"security\", \"billing\", \"troubleshooting\"]\nscenarios = [\"clear\", \"ambiguous\", \"not in docs\", \"wrong assumption\", \"multi-step\"]\npersonas = [\"new user\", \"expert\", \"non-native speaker\", \"in a hurry\"]\n\ntuples = random.sample(list(itertools.product(features, scenarios, personas)), 20)\nPROMPT = \"\"\"Write one realistic message a user might send to a documentation assistant\nfor &lt;product&gt;. Feature: {f}. Scenario: {s}. Persona: {p}.\nWrite only the user's message, in the style that persona would use.\"\"\"\n\ncases = []\nfor f, s, p in tuples:\n    text = llm(PROMPT.format(f=f, s=s, p=p))   # your client from the APIs module\n    cases.append({\"input\": text, \"tags\": [f\"feature:{f}\", f\"scenario:{s}\", f\"persona:{p}\"],\n                  \"source\": \"synthetic\"})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three rules: generate only the \u003cb>inputs\u003c/b>, never the expected outputs (you run them through your real system and grade the results); ground them in real system data where possible (a real product name, an order number that does not exist for a \"not found\" case); and read every generated case before it goes into the dataset.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Balance, difficulty and clarity\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Test both directions.\u003c/b> Anthropic describes building web-search evals for Claude.ai with queries where the model should search and queries where it should answer from knowledge. If you only test the first, you optimise towards a system that searches for everything.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Include hard cases.\u003c/b> A dataset the system already passes at 100% teaches nothing. Keep failing cases in it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Make every case unambiguous.\u003c/b> A good case is one where two domain experts would independently reach the same pass or fail verdict. If they would not, refine the case or its notes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Remove near-duplicates.\u003c/b> Fifty variants of one question inflate one behaviour's weight.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Splits, versioning and hygiene\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep a \u003cb>dev split\u003c/b> you iterate against and a \u003cb>test split\u003c/b> you look at rarely, so you do not overfit prompts to the cases you stare at.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Version the dataset in git (JSONL diffs well) or in a platform such as Langfuse, LangSmith or Braintrust datasets, and record which dataset version produced each score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Scrub personal data from production traces before they become test cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Refresh regularly. Products, documents and users change, and a \"gold\" set from last year slowly stops representing today's traffic.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How big?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with 20 to 50 cases from real failures; early changes have large effects that small sets detect. Grow to 100 or more as the system matures. The statistics lesson shows why: with 100 cases at an 80% pass rate, the 95% confidence interval is roughly plus or minus 8 points, too wide to detect small improvements.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you create 100 eval cases from real traffic plus targeted synthetic cases?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sample about 55 production traces at random and stratified by feature, scrubbing personal data. Add about 25 confirmed failures from error analysis, bug reports and thumbs-down feedback. Generate about 20 synthetic inputs by enumerating dimension tuples (feature, scenario, persona) and having an LLM write a natural query for each, focusing on rare scenarios such as \"not in docs\" or \"wrong assumption\". For every case write a reference answer or must-include and must-not-include grading notes, add tags and a source, read and fix each one, split roughly 70 dev and 30 test, and commit the JSONL with a version.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should you generate only user inputs synthetically, not the expected outputs?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The point of the eval is to grade what your real system produces. If an LLM also writes the \"correct\" outputs, you are testing agreement with another model's guesses, and errors in those references silently become your definition of correct. Run synthetic inputs through your system and grade the outputs against notes a human has checked.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why include cases where the system should not do something?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One-sided datasets cause one-sided optimisation. If every case rewards searching, calling a tool or answering, a change that makes the system do it always will score well while hurting real users. Balanced cases catch over-triggering as well as under-triggering.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why keep a test split you rarely look at?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you iterate prompts against the same cases you inspect, you gradually tune to their quirks. A held-out split gives an honest estimate of how the change performs on cases it was not tuned for.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:code-based-assertions":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>code-based assertion\u003c/b> is a plain function that inspects an output and returns pass or fail, ideally with a reason. Assertions are fast, free, deterministic and never drift, so use them for everything a rule can decide: format, schema, citations, length, required facts, forbidden content and tool-call arguments. Save LLM judges for what genuinely needs judgement.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What code can check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example rule\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical implementation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Format\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output parses as JSON and matches a schema\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> plus a Pydantic model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Has a \"Steps\" section; under 200 words\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regex, word count\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Citations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>At least one \u003ccode>[n]\u003c/code>; every \u003ccode>[n]\u003c/code> refers to a retrieved document\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regex plus set comparison\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Required content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mentions \"30 days\" for refund questions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Case-insensitive search, normalised\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Forbidden content\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No competitor names, emails, card numbers, \"As an AI model\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regex list\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Called \u003ccode>create_ticket\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>cluster\u003c/code> argument\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inspect the tool-call JSON\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Generated SQL runs; generated code passes tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run it in a sandbox\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Budgets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Under 3 s, under $0.01\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read latency and usage from the trace\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Many failure modes that sound like they need a judge are partly checkable by code. \"The answer must be grounded\" needs a judge, but \"every citation points to a document that was actually retrieved\" is a set comparison.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Three checks in Python\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, re\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nclass AtlasAnswer(BaseModel):\n    answer: str\n    citations: list[int]\n    confidence: Literal[\"high\", \"low\"]\n\ndef check_format(raw: str) -&gt; tuple[bool, str]:\n    try:\n        AtlasAnswer.model_validate_json(raw)\n        return True, \"valid\"\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return False, f\"schema error: {e.errors()[0]['msg']}\"\n\nCITE = re.compile(r\"\\[(\\d+)\\]\")\n\ndef check_citations(text: str, retrieved_ids: list[int]) -&gt; tuple[bool, str]:\n    cited = {int(n) for n in CITE.findall(text)}\n    if not cited:\n        return False, \"no citations\"\n    unknown = cited - set(retrieved_ids)\n    if unknown:\n        return False, f\"cites sources that were not retrieved: {sorted(unknown)}\"\n    return True, f\"{len(cited)} valid citations\"\n\nFORBIDDEN = [\n    (re.compile(r\"\\b(?:acme cloud|globex)\\b\", re.I), \"competitor name\"),\n    (re.compile(r\"[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[\\w.]+\"), \"email address\"),\n    (re.compile(r\"\\b(?:\\d[ -]?){13,16}\\b\"), \"card-like number\"),\n    (re.compile(r\"\\bas an ai(?: language)? model\\b\", re.I), \"boilerplate disclaimer\"),\n]\n\ndef check_forbidden(text: str) -&gt; tuple[bool, str]:\n    hits = [label for pattern, label in FORBIDDEN if pattern.search(text)]\n    return (not hits, \"clean\" if not hits else \"found: \" + \", \".join(hits))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each function returns a reason as well as a verdict. When a CI run fails at 2 a.m., \"cites sources that were not retrieved: [7]\" saves an hour.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Running them over a dataset with pytest\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, pytest\nfrom atlas import answer_question   # your system under test\n\nCASES = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"evals/cases.jsonl\")]\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"case\", CASES, ids=[c[\"id\"] for c in CASES])\ndef test_citations(case):\n    result = answer_question(case[\"input\"])\n    ok, why = check_citations(result.text, result.retrieved_ids)\n    assert ok, why\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The same checks in promptfoo\u003c/h4>\u003cp>promptfoo (an open-source eval CLI; it agreed to be acquired by OpenAI in March 2026 and remains open source) expresses checks as YAML assertions. Built-in types include \u003ccode>equals\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>contains\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>icontains\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>regex\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>is-json\u003c/code> (with an optional JSON schema), \u003ccode>contains-json\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>javascript\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>python\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>latency\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>cost\u003c/code>, and any type can be negated with a \u003ccode>not-\u003c/code> prefix.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">yaml\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>prompts: [file://prompts/atlas_answer.txt]\nproviders: [openai:chat:&lt;model&gt;]\ntests:\n  - vars: {question: \"How long is the refund window?\"}\n    assert:\n      - type: is-json\n      - type: icontains\n        value: \"30 days\"\n      - type: not-icontains\n        value: \"as an ai model\"\n      - type: python\n        value: file://checks.py:assert_citations\n      - type: latency\n        threshold: 3000\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The Python file defines \u003ccode>assert_citations(output, context)\u003c/code> and returns a bool, a score or a dict with \u003ccode>pass\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>score\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code>; test variables are available as \u003ccode>context[\"vars\"]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Design rules\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>One assertion per failure mode\u003c/b>, named after it, so results read like your error-analysis table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Test the tests.\u003c/b> For every checker keep at least one output that must pass and one that must fail, and run them as unit tests. A regex that never fires looks exactly like a system that never fails.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Normalise before matching:\u003c/b> lower-case, collapse whitespace, strip Markdown. Accept reasonable variants (\"30 days\", \"thirty days\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Avoid exact-match on free text.\u003c/b> It fails on harmless rephrasing and trains you to ignore red results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reuse at runtime.\u003c/b> The same format and forbidden-content checks can run as guardrails on every live response (covered in the Security module) and as online monitors (Observability module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you write deterministic checks for format, citations and forbidden content?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Format: parse the output with a Pydantic model (or a JSON schema) and fail with the first validation error. Citations: extract \u003ccode>[n]\u003c/code> markers with a regex, fail if there are none, and fail if any cited id is not in the set of documents actually retrieved for that request. Forbidden content: keep a list of compiled regexes for competitor names, emails, card-like numbers and boilerplate phrases, and fail with the labels of any that match. Each returns a verdict plus a reason, runs in pytest over the dataset or as promptfoo \u003ccode>python\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>is-json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>not-icontains\u003c/code> assertions, and has its own known-pass and known-fail examples.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should every assertion have its own known-pass and known-fail examples?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A broken checker is silent: a regex with a typo never fires and the suite stays green. Small unit tests that show the checker passing a good output and failing a bad one prove it can detect the failure it is named after.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An answer to refund questions must state the 30-day window. Code or judge?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start with code: a normalised, case-insensitive check for \"30 days\" or \"thirty days\" on cases tagged as refund questions. It is cheap and deterministic. Only add a judge if error analysis shows many correct answers phrased in ways a rule cannot capture.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:llm-as-judge-and-its-biases":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>LLM judge\u003c/b> (LLM-as-a-judge) is a model prompted to grade another model's output against written criteria. It scales something close to human judgement to thousands of cases, but it has systematic biases and can be confidently wrong. Write one narrow, binary judge per failure mode, and trust it only after you have measured its agreement with human labels.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three ways to use a judge\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Format\u003c/th>\u003cth>Question asked\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Direct (pointwise)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does this output pass the criterion?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Objective-ish checks: groundedness, policy, instruction following\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reference-based\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does this output match the reference answer?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Questions with a known answer that can be phrased many ways\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pairwise\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which of these two outputs is better?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subjective quality: tone, helpfulness, writing (a later lesson)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What the research found\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In 2023, Zheng and colleagues tested strong models as judges on MT-Bench (a multi-turn question set) and Chatbot Arena votes. GPT-4 reached over 80% agreement with human preferences, the same level at which humans agree with each other; in one setup that excluded ties it agreed with experts 85% of the time against 81% human-to-human agreement. They also documented the biases you must design around: the judges they tested preferred the answer in a particular position, favoured longer answers (on a test with padded answers, two judges preferred the longer one over 90% of the time) and rated their own model's answers higher. Those are 2023 models; do not assume today's judges are free of these effects. Measure them on your data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Biases and fixes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Bias\u003c/th>\u003cth>What happens\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mitigation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Position\u003c/td>\u003ctd>In pairwise, prefers whichever answer comes first (or second)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Judge both orders; count only consistent verdicts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Verbosity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefers longer, more elaborate answers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Criteria that reward concision; check verdict against length\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Self-preference\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rates its own model family's outputs higher\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Use a judge from a different provider or family\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Surface authority\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Impressed by confident tone, Markdown, citations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check citations in code; tell the judge style is irrelevant\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Leniency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Passes too much, especially with vague criteria\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary verdict with explicit fail conditions and fail examples\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weak verification\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cannot check maths, code or facts it lacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give it the reference, the retrieved documents or test results\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A real judge prompt\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One judge per failure mode. Here is a judge for Atlas's most common failure from error analysis, unsupported claims:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>You are grading one response from Atlas, an assistant that answers questions about\n&lt;product&gt; using retrieved documentation.\n\nCheck ONE failure mode: UNSUPPORTED CLAIM.\nThe response FAILS if it states any specific factual claim (a number, version,\nflag, setting name, command, default value or behaviour) that is not supported by\nthe retrieved documents below. Paraphrasing a document is fine. Generic knowledge\nany engineer would know does not count as a claim. A response that says it does\nnot know, or asks a clarifying question, PASSES this check.\nIgnore tone, length and formatting: they are graded elsewhere.\n\nExample 1\nDocuments: \"Keys are rotated with `atlas keys rotate`. Old keys stay valid for 24 hours.\"\nResponse: \"Run `atlas keys rotate --force`; the old key is revoked immediately.\"\nCritique: The `--force` flag and immediate revocation appear in no document; the\ndocs say old keys stay valid for 24 hours.\nVerdict: FAIL\n\nExample 2\nDocuments: \"Backups run nightly at 02:00 UTC and are kept for 14 days.\"\nResponse: \"Backups happen every night (2 am UTC) and you can restore from the last two weeks.\"\nCritique: Both claims paraphrase the document.\nVerdict: PASS\n\n&lt;documents&gt;\n{documents}\n&lt;/documents&gt;\n&lt;question&gt;{question}&lt;/question&gt;\n&lt;response&gt;{response}&lt;/response&gt;\n\nList each specific claim in the response and whether a document supports it.\nThen answer with JSON only:\n{\"critique\": \"&lt;2 to 4 sentences naming any unsupported claim&gt;\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\" or \"FAIL\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Why it looks like this:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>One failure mode, binary verdict.\u003c/b> \"Rate helpfulness 1 to 5\" gives numbers nobody can act on and annotators cannot agree on. Pass or fail on a precise definition can be validated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Explicit definitions and exclusions\u003c/b>, including what passes (\"says it does not know\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Few-shot examples taken from your own labelled traces\u003c/b>, with a critique each, so the judge learns your standard.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Critique before verdict\u003c/b>, so the model reasons about the evidence before committing, and so you can read why it decided.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The same context the system had.\u003c/b> A groundedness judge without the retrieved documents is guessing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Calling it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\n\ndef judge_unsupported(question: str, documents: str, response: str) -&gt; dict:\n    # replace(), not format(): the prompt contains literal JSON braces\n    prompt = (JUDGE_PROMPT.replace(\"{documents}\", documents)\n              .replace(\"{question}\", question).replace(\"{response}\", response))\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"&lt;judge-model&gt;\",          # ideally a different family from the system under test\n        temperature=0,\n        response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"},\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n    )\n    out = json.loads(r.choices[0].message.content)\n    assert out[\"verdict\"] in (\"PASS\", \"FAIL\")\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Measuring agreement, briefly\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Label 50 to 100 outputs yourself (pass or fail, with a short critique), run the judge on the same outputs, and compare. Report how many real failures the judge catches and how many good outputs it passes, not just overall agreement. The next lesson does this properly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start with a strong judge model to establish that the criterion is learnable; consider a cheaper model later, re-validated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ready-made metrics from libraries (\"coherence\", \"answer relevance\") are a fine starting point for exploration but were not validated on your task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Budget the cost: a judge call per case per trial. With 300 cases, 3 trials and two judges you make 1,800 judge calls per run.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you build a judge and measure its agreement with your own labels?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pick one failure mode from error analysis and write a binary judge prompt with a precise fail definition, exclusions, two or three labelled examples with critiques, the context the system saw, and a JSON output with critique then verdict. Label 50 to 100 different outputs yourself as pass or fail, run the judge on them, and compare: compute how many of your fails it catches (true positive rate), how many of your passes it passes (true negative rate) and overall agreement, then read every disagreement to improve the prompt.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is position bias and how do you neutralise it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In pairwise judging, a model tends to prefer the answer in one position regardless of content. Run every comparison twice with the order swapped and count a win only when both orders agree; treat disagreement as a tie.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should a judge write a critique before giving its verdict?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Generating the reasoning first conditions the verdict on an explicit look at the evidence, which tends to improve accuracy, and the critique lets you audit disagreements and turn good critiques into few-shot examples.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a judge from a different model family often preferred?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Judges show self-preference, rating outputs from their own family more favourably. A judge from a different provider removes that systematic bias from your comparisons.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:aligning-judges-with-human-labels":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A judge is a classifier, so validate it like one. Have a domain expert label outputs, split the labels into examples, a development set and a held-out test set, iterate the judge prompt on the development set, and confirm once on the test set. Report the \u003cb>true positive rate\u003c/b> (failures caught), the \u003cb>true negative rate\u003c/b> (good outputs passed) and \u003cb>Cohen's kappa\u003c/b> (agreement beyond chance), because raw agreement can look excellent for a useless judge.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The process\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Expert labels 100-200 outputs\u003c/b>\u003csmall>pass or fail plus critique\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Split\u003c/b>\u003csmall>examples, dev, test\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Draft judge prompt\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Measure on dev\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Read disagreements and fix\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Final check on test\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Husain calls this \u003cb>critique shadowing\u003c/b>: the judge learns to shadow one principal domain expert's pass or fail decisions and critiques.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Splits\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A typical split for 200 labelled outputs: 20% as a pool of few-shot examples for the prompt, 40% development, 40% test. Make sure both labels are well represented: the evals FAQ recommends at least 50 passing and 50 failing examples, and 100 to 200 labelled examples per failure mode. If failures are rare in production, oversample them for labelling.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Never put test examples into the prompt. If you do, you are grading the judge on its own homework.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The metrics, with a worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat \"the output fails\" as the positive class, because catching failures is the judge's job. You run version 1 of the judge on 100 held-out test outputs that your expert labelled: 30 fails and 70 passes.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Expert: FAIL\u003c/th>\u003cth>Expert: PASS\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Judge: FAIL\u003c/td>\u003ctd>24 (true positive)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7 (false positive)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Judge: PASS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6 (false negative)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>63 (true negative)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>TPR (recall on failures) = 24 / 30 = 0.80     the judge catches 80% of real failures\nTNR                      = 63 / 70 = 0.90     it passes 90% of good outputs\nObserved agreement p_o   = (24 + 63) / 100 = 0.87\n\nChance agreement p_e = P(expert FAIL) × P(judge FAIL) + P(expert PASS) × P(judge PASS)\n                     = 0.30 × 0.31 + 0.70 × 0.69 = 0.093 + 0.483 = 0.576\nCohen's kappa = (p_o - p_e) / (1 - p_e) = (0.87 - 0.576) / 0.424 = 0.69\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After two rounds of fixes, version 2 gets 27 of 30 failures and 66 of 70 passes: TPR 0.90, TNR 0.94, agreement 0.93 and kappa (0.93 - 0.576) / 0.424 = 0.83.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A common rough reading of kappa (Landis and Koch, 1977) calls 0.61 to 0.80 \"substantial\" and above 0.80 \"almost perfect\". Use it as a guide, not a law.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Why raw agreement misleads\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Suppose only 5 of 100 outputs are real failures. A judge that always says PASS agrees with the expert 95% of the time. Its TPR is 0 (it catches nothing), and its kappa is 0, because 95% is exactly the agreement you would expect by chance. Always report TPR and TNR, and decide which matters more: a safety judge needs a high TPR even at the cost of false alarms; a judge that pages a human needs a high TNR, or reviewers drown in false alarms.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Correcting a measured failure rate\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Once validated, you run the judge on 1,000 production traces and it flags 20% as failing. Because the judge has known error rates, the true rate is not 20%. The Rogan-Gladen correction, a classic estimator from epidemiology, adjusts for them:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>true rate ≈ (observed rate + TNR - 1) / (TPR + TNR - 1)\n          = (0.20 + 0.943 - 1) / (0.90 + 0.943 - 1) = 0.143 / 0.843 ≈ 0.17\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>So about 17% of traces really fail. Remember the TPR itself is uncertain: 27 of 30 has a 95% Wilson interval of roughly 74% to 97%. More labelled failures narrow it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Fixing disagreements\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read every disagreement on the dev set and ask which of these it is:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>The label is wrong or the criterion changed\u003c/b> (criteria drift). Update the definition and re-label. This is common in early rounds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The judge lacked context\u003c/b>, such as the retrieved documents or the user's earlier turns. Pass it in.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The definition is vague\u003c/b> at exactly this kind of case. Add an explicit rule or an exclusion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The judge needs an example.\u003c/b> Add one from the example pool, never from the test set.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Only after manual fixes plateau, consider automatic prompt optimisers such as DSPy or GEPA, which propose prompt changes and keep those that raise agreement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def judge_report(expert: list[str], judge: list[str]) -&gt; dict:\n    pairs = list(zip(expert, judge))\n    tp = sum(e == \"FAIL\" and j == \"FAIL\" for e, j in pairs)\n    tn = sum(e == \"PASS\" and j == \"PASS\" for e, j in pairs)\n    fp = sum(e == \"PASS\" and j == \"FAIL\" for e, j in pairs)\n    fn = sum(e == \"FAIL\" and j == \"PASS\" for e, j in pairs)\n    n = len(pairs)\n    po = (tp + tn) / n\n    pe = ((tp + fn) / n) * ((tp + fp) / n) + ((tn + fp) / n) * ((tn + fn) / n)\n    return {\"tpr\": tp / (tp + fn), \"tnr\": tn / (tn + fp), \"agreement\": po,\n            \"kappa\": (po - pe) / (1 - pe), \"n\": n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Re-validate whenever you change the judge model, the prompt or the system's output style.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep labelling a small sample of production verdicts each month (20 to 50) to catch drift.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the judge will not converge, the criterion is usually too broad. Split it into two narrower judges.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you iterate a judge prompt until it reaches 90% agreement on a held-out set?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Have a domain expert label 100 to 200 outputs pass or fail with critiques, including at least 50 of each. Split them into an example pool (about 20%), a dev set (40%) and a test set (40%). Write the judge prompt with few-shot examples from the pool only, run it on dev, compute agreement, TPR, TNR and kappa, read every disagreement, and fix labels, context, definitions or examples. Repeat until dev agreement is above 90% with acceptable TPR and TNR, then run once on the untouched test set and report those numbers. If test agreement is far below dev, you overfitted: relabel or add data rather than tuning on the test set.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A judge agrees with your labels on 95 of 100 cases. Why might it still be useless?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If only 5 of the 100 are real failures, a judge that always says PASS also scores 95%, with a true positive rate of zero and kappa of zero. You need TPR, TNR and kappa to know whether it catches failures.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Compute Cohen's kappa for 100 cases where the expert marks 30 as FAIL, the judge marks 31 as FAIL, and they agree on 87.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>p_o = 0.87. p_e = 0.30 × 0.31 + 0.70 × 0.69 = 0.576. Kappa = (0.87 - 0.576) / (1 - 0.576) = 0.294 / 0.424 ≈ 0.69.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your validated judge (TPR 0.90, TNR 0.94) flags 25% of production traces. Roughly what is the true failure rate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Using the Rogan-Gladen correction with TNR 0.943: (0.25 + 0.943 - 1) / (0.90 + 0.943 - 1) = 0.193 / 0.843 ≈ 0.23, so about 23%.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:pairwise-comparisons":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Instead of grading each output on its own, show a judge (or a person) two outputs for the same input and ask which is better. Relative judgements are easier and more consistent than absolute scores, so pairwise comparison is the standard way to choose between two prompts or models on open-ended quality. Run every pair in both orders to cancel position bias, and report the win rate with a confidence interval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When pairwise beats pointwise\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask a judge to rate one answer's helpfulness from 1 to 5 and the scores wander. Ask \"which of these two is more helpful?\" and both humans and models answer more consistently. Eugene Yan's survey notes that studies find pairwise comparison gives more stable results and smaller gaps between LLM and human judgements than direct scoring, for subjective qualities such as tone, persuasiveness and coherence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pairwise is the wrong tool when:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Correctness is objective.\u003c/b> Use a reference, code or a pointwise judge. Two wrong answers can still produce a \"winner\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>You need absolute quality.\u003c/b> A 70% win rate says B is better than A, not that B is good enough to ship. Keep pointwise pass rates alongside.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The procedure\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Run the same inputs through system A (current prompt) and system B (candidate).\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each input, ask the judge to compare the two outputs against explicit criteria, allowing a tie.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ask again with the order swapped.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If both orders pick the same answer, it wins. If both say tie, it is a tie. If the verdict flips with the order, the judge is reacting to position, not content: count it as a tie.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A pairwise judge prompt:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>You are comparing two answers from a documentation assistant to the same question.\n\n&lt;question&gt;{question}&lt;/question&gt;\n&lt;documents&gt;{documents}&lt;/documents&gt;\n&lt;answer_1&gt;{answer_1}&lt;/answer_1&gt;\n&lt;answer_2&gt;{answer_2}&lt;/answer_2&gt;\n\nJudge by these criteria, in priority order:\n1. Correct and supported by the documents.\n2. Answers exactly what was asked, including constraints such as version or OS.\n3. Easy to act on: the key fact or step comes first, no padding.\nLength is not a virtue. Formatting alone is not a reason to prefer an answer.\nIf the answers are equally good or equally bad on these criteria, choose TIE.\n\nOutput JSON only: {\"reason\": \"&lt;two sentences&gt;\", \"winner\": \"1\" or \"2\" or \"TIE\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def compare(case, out_a, out_b) -&gt; str:\n    v1 = judge_pair(case, out_a, out_b)   # A shown first; returns \"1\", \"2\" or \"TIE\"\n    v2 = judge_pair(case, out_b, out_a)   # B shown first\n    if v1 == \"1\" and v2 == \"2\":\n        return \"A\"\n    if v1 == \"2\" and v2 == \"1\":\n        return \"B\"\n    if v1 == v2 == \"TIE\":\n        return \"TIE\"\n    return \"INCONSISTENT\"                 # verdict depends on position: treat as a tie\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example: prompt v1 (A) against prompt v2 (B)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You compare the two prompts on 200 questions.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Outcome\u003c/th>\u003cth>Count\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>B wins in both orders\u003c/td>\u003ctd>78\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A wins in both orders\u003c/td>\u003ctd>52\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tie in both orders\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Verdict flips with order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Decisive comparisons     = 78 + 52 = 130\nB's win rate (decisive)  = 78 / 130 = 60.0%\n95% Wilson interval      = 51.4% to 68.0%\nSign test, two-sided     : P(a split at least this uneven | A and B equally good) = 0.028\nWin rate, ties as half   = (78 + (20 + 50) / 2) / 200 = 113 / 200 = 56.5%\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from math import comb, sqrt\n\ndef wilson(k: int, n: int, z: float = 1.96) -&gt; tuple[float, float]:\n    p = k / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / (1 + z * z / n)\n    half = z * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / (1 + z * z / n)\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\ndef sign_test(wins_a: int, wins_b: int) -&gt; float:\n    n, k = wins_a + wins_b, max(wins_a, wins_b)\n    return min(1.0, 2 * sum(comb(n, i) for i in range(k, n + 1)) / 2 ** n)\n\nprint(wilson(78, 130), sign_test(52, 78))   # (0.514, 0.680), 0.028\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Reading it: B is better, and the interval excludes 50%, but only just; on 130 decisive comparisons a true win rate anywhere from about 51% to 68% is plausible. Also notice that 50 of 200 verdicts (25%) flipped with order. That is a strongly position-biased judge: sharpen the criteria and add examples before trusting finer comparisons.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Ranking many systems: Elo and Bradley-Terry\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With more than two candidates, fit a rating per system from all pairwise results. The \u003cb>Bradley-Terry model\u003c/b> (which Chatbot Arena uses, in the same spirit as chess Elo ratings) assumes the chance that system i beats system j depends only on their rating gap:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>P(i beats j) = 1 / (1 + 10 ^ ((R_j - R_i) / 400))\ngap of  50 points -&gt; 57% expected win rate\ngap of 100 points -&gt; 64%\ngap of 200 points -&gt; 76%\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Arena-style leaderboards also report confidence intervals from bootstrapping (re-fitting on resampled votes), because ratings for systems with few votes are uncertain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use pairwise for prompt rewrites, model swaps and writing-quality changes; keep pointwise checks for correctness and policy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Watch for verbosity bias: compare the average length of winners and losers. If winners are much longer, your judge may be rewarding length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Have a human check 20 to 30 pairs, especially the flips, before trusting the judge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the tie option. Forcing a choice between two equally good answers adds noise.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you compare two prompts pairwise and compute win rates?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run the same inputs through both prompts, then have a judge compare each pair against explicit criteria with a tie option, once in each order. Count a win only when both orders agree, and treat flips as ties. Report the win rate over decisive comparisons with a confidence interval and a sign test, plus the tie and flip counts: for example 78 wins against 52 out of 200 gives 60.0% (95% Wilson interval 51.4% to 68.0%, sign test p = 0.028), or 56.5% if ties count as half.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a verdict that flips when you swap the order treated as a tie?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A flip means the judge preferred whichever answer was in a particular position, so the verdict reflects position bias rather than content. Counting it as a win for either side would add bias; counting it as a tie neutralises it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does a pairwise win rate not tell you?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whether either system is good enough. Both can be poor, and a high win rate only means one is preferred over the other. You still need pointwise pass rates for correctness, safety and other absolute requirements.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Two systems differ by 100 Elo-style rating points. What win rate does the model predict for the stronger one?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>1 / (1 + 10^(-100/400)) ≈ 0.64, so about 64%.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:evaluating-agents-outcomes-and-trajectories":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent is judged on two things: the \u003cb>outcome\u003c/b> (is the world in the right state at the end: ticket created, tests passing, refund issued?) and the \u003cb>trajectory\u003c/b> (how it got there: tool calls, errors, steps, cost, policy violations). Grade outcomes first, add trajectory checks only for what matters, run several trials per task because agents are not deterministic, and report \u003cb>pass@k\u003c/b> or \u003cb>pass^k\u003c/b> depending on whether one success is enough or every run must succeed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Outcome versus trajectory\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Outcome\u003c/th>\u003cth>Trajectory\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Question\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did the task get done?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Was it done safely and efficiently?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Evidence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Database rows, files, test results, API state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The trace: tool calls, arguments, errors, turns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Example check\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A ticket exists for cluster \u003ccode>prod-eu\u003c/code> with priority P2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No write tool called before the user confirmed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Typical grader\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code for counts and rules, LLM judge for quality of interaction\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An agent can \u003ci>say\u003c/i> \"your ticket has been created\" while no ticket exists. Grade the world, not the agent's claim.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Do not over-specify the path\u003c/h4>\u003cp>It is tempting to assert an exact sequence of tool calls. Anthropic found that approach too rigid: agents regularly find valid approaches the eval designer did not anticipate, and exact-path tests punish them. Grade what the agent produced, and constrain the path only where it matters: safety rules (verify identity before a refund), budgets (at most 12 steps) and known waste (calling the same tool with the same arguments three times).\u003c/p>\u003cp>For multi-part tasks, give \u003cb>partial credit\u003c/b>. A support agent that verifies the customer and finds the order but fails to issue the refund is better than one that fails at the first step, and your scores should show it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Grading code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom jsonschema import Draft202012Validator\n\nWRITE_TOOLS = {\"create_ticket\", \"restart_service\"}\n\ndef grade_outcome(db, case) -&gt; bool:\n    \"\"\"Check the world, not what the agent said.\"\"\"\n    row = db.execute(\"SELECT cluster, priority FROM tickets WHERE request_id = ?\",\n                     (case[\"request_id\"],)).fetchone()\n    return row is not None and tuple(row) == (case[\"expected\"][\"cluster\"], case[\"expected\"][\"priority\"])\n\ndef grade_trajectory(steps: list[dict], schemas: dict[str, dict], max_steps: int = 12) -&gt; dict:\n    calls = [s for s in steps if s[\"type\"] == \"tool_call\"]\n    unknown = [c for c in calls if c[\"name\"] not in schemas]\n    invalid = [c for c in calls if c[\"name\"] in schemas\n               and not Draft202012Validator(schemas[c[\"name\"]]).is_valid(c[\"args\"])]\n    signature = Counter((c[\"name\"], json.dumps(c[\"args\"], sort_keys=True)) for c in calls)\n    repeated = sum(n - 1 for n in signature.values())\n    tool_errors = sum(1 for s in steps if s[\"type\"] == \"tool_result\" and s.get(\"is_error\"))\n    confirmed, unconfirmed_writes = False, 0\n    for s in steps:\n        if s[\"type\"] == \"user\" and s.get(\"confirms_action\"):\n            confirmed = True\n        if s[\"type\"] == \"tool_call\" and s[\"name\"] in WRITE_TOOLS and not confirmed:\n            unconfirmed_writes += 1\n    return {\"unknown_tools\": len(unknown), \"invalid_args\": len(invalid),\n            \"repeated_calls\": repeated, \"tool_errors\": tool_errors,\n            \"unconfirmed_writes\": unconfirmed_writes, \"steps\": len(steps),\n            \"within_budget\": len(steps) &lt;= max_steps}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A task passes when the outcome is right \u003cb>and\u003c/b> hard rules hold (\u003ccode>unconfirmed_writes == 0\u003c/code>). The other numbers are tracked as metrics: a change that keeps the pass rate but doubles repeated calls has made the agent slower and more expensive.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Non-determinism: trials, pass@k and pass^k\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Run each task several times (each run is a \u003cb>trial\u003c/b>). Two summaries answer different questions:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>pass@k\u003c/b>: the probability that at least one of k trials succeeds. Right for tools where a human or a test picks the one good attempt, such as generating code candidates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>pass^k\u003c/b> (pass-hat-k, from tau-bench): the probability that all k trials succeed. Right for customer-facing agents, where every user must get a correct result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Per-trial success p = 0.75, k = 3\npass^3 = 0.75^3           = 0.42\npass@3 = 1 - (1 - 0.75)^3 = 0.98\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With n trials of which c succeeded, the standard unbiased estimates are:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>pass@k ≈ 1 - C(n - c, k) / C(n, k)\npass^k ≈ C(c, k) / C(n, k)\n\nn = 10 trials, c = 6 successes, k = 3:\npass@3 = 1 - C(4, 3) / C(10, 3) = 1 - 4 / 120 = 0.97\npass^3 = C(6, 3) / C(10, 3)     = 20 / 120    = 0.17\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The tau-bench paper showed why this matters: even strong function-calling agents of 2024 succeeded on under 50% of tasks, and in the retail domain pass^8 fell below 25%. An agent that works \"most of the time\" fails often across many users.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Environments and simulated users\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Isolate every trial.\u003c/b> Start from a clean database, file system or container. Shared state causes correlated failures and can even leak answers (Anthropic saw a model read the git history of earlier trials).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Simulate the user\u003c/b> for conversational agents: a second model plays a persona with a hidden goal, as in tau-bench and tau2-bench.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check impossible tasks.\u003c/b> If a strong model scores 0% on a task across many trials, the task or grader is usually broken.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inspect (with Docker sandboxes and agent support), Harbor (containerised agent tasks; Terminal-Bench ships through it) and tau2-bench are common harnesses. For trajectory quality that code cannot judge (\"did it ask a sensible clarifying question?\"), use a validated LLM judge over the transcript. Track cost and steps per task alongside success.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you grade an agent on task success and on tool-use errors?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Task success: after each trial, inspect the environment's final state with code (the ticket row exists with the right fields, the tests pass), ignoring what the agent claims. Tool-use errors: parse the trace and count calls to unknown tools, calls whose arguments fail the tool's JSON schema, repeated identical calls, tool errors and policy violations such as write actions before user confirmation, plus total steps against a budget. Pass a trial only if the outcome is right and hard rules hold, track the counts as metrics, and run several trials per task to report pass@k or pass^k.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent succeeds on a task 90% of the time per trial. What are pass^5 and pass@5?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>pass^5 = 0.9^5 ≈ 0.59, so all five runs succeed only 59% of the time. pass@5 = 1 - 0.1^5 = 0.99999, so at least one succeeds almost always.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why grade outcomes rather than the exact sequence of tool calls?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Agents often find valid paths the designer did not anticipate, so exact-sequence tests fail good runs and become brittle. Checking the final state rewards any correct solution, while a few targeted trajectory rules still catch unsafe or wasteful behaviour.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:eval-frameworks-promptfoo-inspect-and-deepeval":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Eval frameworks handle the plumbing: loading datasets, calling models in parallel, running graders, caching, storing and comparing results. \u003cb>promptfoo\u003c/b> is configuration-first (YAML plus a CLI, strong in CI), \u003cb>Inspect\u003c/b> is code-first and built for agentic and research-grade evals, \u003cb>DeepEval\u003c/b> feels like pytest, and \u003cb>platforms\u003c/b> such as Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse and provider evals add hosted datasets, experiments and dashboards. Pick one quickly and spend your effort on cases and graders.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The main options, as of late 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003cth>Shape\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>promptfoo\u003c/td>\u003ctd>YAML config, Node CLI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many providers, deterministic and model-graded assertions, side-by-side matrix view, red-teaming\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open source; agreed to be acquired by OpenAI in March 2026\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inspect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python: Task = dataset + solver + scorer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agents, tools, Docker sandboxes, epochs, rich log viewer, many published benchmarks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open source, from the UK AI Security Institute\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DeepEval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pytest-style tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>assert_test\u003c/code>, G-Eval and many ready metrics, \u003ccode>deepeval test run\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open source, from Confident AI\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Braintrust\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted platform plus SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Experiments and diffs, \u003ccode>autoevals\u003c/code> scorers, production logging\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commercial\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangSmith\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted platform\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Datasets, evaluators, experiments, online evals; tight LangChain and LangGraph fit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commercial; self-host on enterprise plans\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Langfuse, Phoenix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source platforms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Datasets, experiments and judges attached to traces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Self-hostable; covered in the Observability module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Provider evals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Evals with graders (\u003ccode>string_check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>text_similarity\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>score_model\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>python\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>multi\u003c/code>); the same graders drive reinforcement fine-tuning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tied to one provider\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The same check in three tools\u003c/h4>\u003cp>promptfoo:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">yaml\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>prompts: [file://prompts/atlas_answer.txt]\nproviders: [openai:chat:&lt;model&gt;, anthropic:messages:&lt;model&gt;]\ntests: file://evals/refunds.yaml\ndefaultTest:\n  assert:\n    - type: icontains\n      value: \"30 days\"\n    - type: llm-rubric\n      value: \"States the refund window without inventing conditions not in the policy.\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>DeepEval:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from deepeval import assert_test\nfrom deepeval.metrics import GEval\nfrom deepeval.test_case import LLMTestCase, SingleTurnParams\n\npolicy = GEval(\n    name=\"Refund policy faithfulness\",\n    criteria=\"The actual output states the refund window and invents no conditions absent from the expected output.\",\n    evaluation_params=[SingleTurnParams.ACTUAL_OUTPUT, SingleTurnParams.EXPECTED_OUTPUT],\n    threshold=0.5,\n)\n\ndef test_refund_window():\n    case = LLMTestCase(input=\"How long do I have to return an item?\",\n                       actual_output=atlas.answer(\"How long do I have to return an item?\"),\n                       expected_output=\"Items can be returned within 30 days of delivery.\")\n    assert_test(case, [policy])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>OpenAI grader (JSON):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"type\": \"string_check\", \"name\": \"mentions refund window\",\n \"input\": \"{{sample.output_text}}\", \"reference\": \"30 days\", \"operation\": \"ilike\"}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Porting an eval to Inspect\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inspect evaluates a model through a \u003cb>solver\u003c/b> (the steps that produce an answer, from a single \u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code> to a full agent) and a \u003cb>scorer\u003c/b>. To port Atlas's prompt-level refund eval and compare two models:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># atlas_eval.py\nfrom inspect_ai import Task, task\nfrom inspect_ai.dataset import FieldSpec, json_dataset\nfrom inspect_ai.scorer import includes, model_graded_qa\nfrom inspect_ai.solver import generate, system_message\n\nSYSTEM = open(\"prompts/atlas_system.txt\").read()\n\n@task\ndef atlas_refunds():\n    return Task(\n        dataset=json_dataset(\"evals/refunds.jsonl\",\n                             FieldSpec(input=\"input\", target=\"target\", id=\"id\")),\n        solver=[system_message(SYSTEM), generate()],\n        scorer=[includes(), model_graded_qa(model=\"openai/&lt;judge-model&gt;\")],\n    )\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each JSONL line holds \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>input\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> (the must-include phrase for \u003ccode>includes()\u003c/code>, which \u003ccode>model_graded_qa()\u003c/code> also reads as grading guidance). Your custom Python checks from earlier lessons become scorers with the \u003ccode>@scorer\u003c/code> decorator. Then:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>pip install inspect-ai openai anthropic\ninspect eval atlas_eval.py --model openai/&lt;model&gt; --epochs 3\ninspect eval atlas_eval.py --model anthropic/&lt;model&gt; --epochs 3\ninspect view        # browse both logs, per-sample transcripts and scores\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--epochs 3\u003c/code> runs every sample three times. To evaluate the whole Atlas application rather than a bare model with its prompt, write a custom solver or agent that calls your code.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How to choose\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Mostly prompt-level checks, run in CI by a product team: \u003cb>promptfoo\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Agents, sandboxes, published benchmarks, careful logging: \u003cb>Inspect\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A Python team that wants evals to look like unit tests: \u003cb>DeepEval\u003c/b> or plain \u003cb>pytest\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Many people sharing datasets, experiments and production traces: a \u003cb>platform\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Plain pytest plus a JSONL file is a perfectly respectable answer. Frameworks are only as good as the cases and graders you feed them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you port one eval to Inspect and run it on two models?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Convert the cases to JSONL with \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>input\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> fields; write a \u003ccode>@task\u003c/code> function returning a \u003ccode>Task\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>json_dataset(...)\u003c/code>, a solver list such as \u003ccode>[system_message(SYSTEM), generate()]\u003c/code> and scorers such as \u003ccode>includes()\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>model_graded_qa()\u003c/code> or your own \u003ccode>@scorer\u003c/code>. Run \u003ccode>inspect eval atlas_eval.py --model openai/&lt;model&gt; --epochs 3\u003c/code> and the same with \u003ccode>--model anthropic/&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>, then compare accuracy and per-sample transcripts in \u003ccode>inspect view\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What do OpenAI's graders let you express?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grading rules as JSON: \u003ccode>string_check\u003c/code> (equals, not equals, contains, case-insensitive contains), \u003ccode>text_similarity\u003c/code> (fuzzy match, BLEU, ROUGE, cosine and others against a reference, with a pass threshold), \u003ccode>score_model\u003c/code> (an LLM grader returning a score in a range), \u003ccode>python\u003c/code> (a \u003ccode>grade(sample, item)\u003c/code> function returning a float) and \u003ccode>multi\u003c/code> (combine graders with a formula). They run in the Evals API and as reward functions for reinforcement fine-tuning.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the most common mistake when adopting an eval framework?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Treating its built-in generic metrics as your evaluation. Framework metrics such as \"answer relevance\" were not validated on your task; your cases and graders should still come from error analysis and be checked against human labels.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:benchmark-noise-and-statistical-significance":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An eval score is an estimate from a sample of questions, and model outputs are random, so every score carries noise. Report a confidence interval, compare systems on the \u003cb>same\u003c/b> questions with a paired test, and size the eval for the smallest difference you care about. Detecting a 3-point change reliably takes hundreds to thousands of cases, not fifty.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where the noise comes from\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Which questions you happened to pick.\u003c/b> Your 200 cases are a sample from all the questions users could ask. A different 200 would give a different score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Randomness in outputs.\u003c/b> Sampling temperature, provider-side nondeterminism and tool timing mean the same case can pass on one run and fail on the next. Temperature 0 reduces this but does not guarantee identical outputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Grader noise.\u003c/b> An LLM judge is itself a noisy classifier.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Anthropic's statistics post frames it well: the quantity you care about is the score on the whole \"universe\" of possible questions, and your eval is one sample from it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Confidence interval for one score\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a pass rate p measured on n independent cases, the standard error is:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>SE = sqrt(p × (1 - p) / n)          95% interval ≈ p ± 1.96 × SE\n\np = 0.80:   n =  50 -&gt; ± 11.1 points\n            n = 100 -&gt; ±  7.8 points\n            n = 200 -&gt; ±  5.5 points\n            n = 400 -&gt; ±  3.9 points\nn needed for ± 3 points at 80%: 1.96² × 0.16 / 0.03² ≈ 683\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For small n or rates near 0% or 100%, use the Wilson interval (code in the pairwise lesson); 80 out of 100 gives 71.1% to 86.7%.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Comparing two systems: pair the questions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both systems answer the same questions, so compare them question by question. Only the \u003cb>discordant\u003c/b> questions, where one passes and the other fails, carry information about the difference.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Worked example: 200 questions. System A passes 150 (75%), system B passes 160 (80%). B passes 22 questions that A fails; A passes 12 that B fails; the other 166 agree.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Difference          = (22 - 12) / 200 = +5.0 points\nPaired SE           = sqrt((22 + 12) - (22 - 12)² / 200) / 200 = 0.0289\n95% interval        = 5.0 ± 5.7 points  -&gt;  -0.7 to +10.7\nExact McNemar test  : 22 vs 12 out of 34 discordant, p = 0.12\nUnpaired SE (wrong) = sqrt(0.75×0.25/200 + 0.80×0.20/200) = 0.0417  -&gt;  ± 8.2 points\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Pairing shrinks the interval from about 8.2 to 5.7 points, but a 5-point gain on 200 questions is still not clearly real.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from math import comb, sqrt\n\ndef paired_compare(a: list[bool], b: list[bool]) -&gt; dict:\n    n = len(a)\n    b_only = sum(y and not x for x, y in zip(a, b))\n    a_only = sum(x and not y for x, y in zip(a, b))\n    diff = (b_only - a_only) / n\n    se = sqrt((b_only + a_only) - (b_only - a_only) ** 2 / n) / n\n    m, k = b_only + a_only, max(b_only, a_only)\n    p = min(1.0, 2 * sum(comb(m, i) for i in range(k, m + 1)) / 2 ** m) if m else 1.0\n    return {\"diff\": diff, \"ci95\": (diff - 1.96 * se, diff + 1.96 * se), \"mcnemar_p\": p}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How many cases to detect a 3-point difference?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use a \u003cb>power analysis\u003c/b>: choose the difference you want to detect (3 points), the false-alarm rate (5%) and the chance of detecting a real difference (80% power).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Unpaired (two separate samples), 80% vs 83%:\nn per system ≈ (1.96 × sqrt(2 × 0.815 × 0.185) + 0.84 × sqrt(0.80×0.20 + 0.83×0.17))² / 0.03²\n             ≈ 2,630 cases per system\n\nPaired (same questions), discordance rate d = share of questions where the systems disagree:\nn ≈ (1.96 × sqrt(d) + 0.84 × sqrt(d - 0.03²))² / 0.03²\nd = 0.06 -&gt; about 520 questions\nd = 0.10 -&gt; about 870 questions\nRule of thumb: n ≈ 8 × d / Δ²\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>So detecting 3 points needs roughly 500 to 900 shared questions for typical prompt changes, and far more if you compare on different question sets. Large improvements are cheap to detect: a 15-point jump shows up clearly on 50 cases, which is why small suites work early on.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reducing noise\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pair everything.\u003c/b> Same questions, same order, same grader.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Run several trials per question\u003c/b> and average. Total variance of the mean is roughly (between-question variance + within-question variance ÷ k) ÷ n, so extra trials k shrink only the second term; to shrink the first you need more questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cluster your standard errors\u003c/b> when questions come in groups, for example 10 questions about each of 30 documents. Results within a group are correlated, so 300 questions behave like fewer. With an intra-group correlation of 0.2, the design effect is 1 + (10 - 1) × 0.2 = 2.8, and the effective sample size is 300 / 2.8 ≈ 107.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Beware many comparisons.\u003c/b> Test 20 prompt variants at the 5% level and the chance that at least one looks significant by luck alone is 1 - 0.95^20 ≈ 64%. Confirm a winner on fresh data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Freeze the dataset version\u003c/b> before an experiment, so you are not changing the ruler while measuring.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Report scores like \"78% (95% CI 72 to 84, n = 180)\". In CI, gate on drops larger than the noise, not on any drop. When a difference matters and the interval is too wide, add cases or trials rather than arguing about the point estimate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How many cases do you need to detect a 3-point difference?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It depends on the design. With 80% power at the 5% significance level, comparing 80% with 83% on separate samples needs about 2,600 cases per system. Comparing on the same questions (paired) needs n ≈ (1.96 × sqrt(d) + 0.84 × sqrt(d - 0.0009))² / 0.0009, where d is the share of questions on which the systems disagree: about 520 questions if d = 6% and 870 if d = 10%. Roughly, n ≈ 8 × d / Δ².\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A new prompt scores 84% against the old prompt's 80% on 100 questions. Should you ship it on that evidence?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Not yet. Each score has a 95% interval of roughly plus or minus 7 to 8 points, and an unpaired difference of 4 points has an interval of about plus or minus 10.6 points. Run a paired comparison on the discordant questions, and add cases or trials until the interval excludes zero or is narrow enough to decide.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does running each question three times help, and what does it not fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It averages out output randomness, shrinking the within-question part of the variance. It does not reduce the variance from which questions were sampled; only more questions do that.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a clustered standard error and when do you need one?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An error estimate that accounts for correlation among related questions, such as several questions about the same document or several turns of one conversation. Without it you overstate your effective sample size and report intervals that are too narrow.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:evals/evals-in-ci-and-online-evaluation":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Offline evals\u003c/b> run in CI before a change ships; \u003cb>online evals\u003c/b> score live production traffic after it ships. CI gives fast, cheap regression protection on every prompt, model or code change; online evaluation catches what your dataset never imagined. Mature teams run both, and add A/B tests for big changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Tiered suites\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Running every judge on every case on every commit is slow and expensive. Split the suite into tiers.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Tier\u003c/th>\u003cth>Trigger\u003c/th>\u003cth>Contents\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time and cost\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gate\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Smoke\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every pull request touching prompts, AI code or evals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 to 50 cases, code checks, maybe one cheap judge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Under 5 minutes, cents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regression cases 100%; no crash\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Merge to main, nightly, any model or provider change\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All cases, code checks and validated judges, 3 trials\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20 to 60 minutes, a few dollars\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No metric drops beyond noise\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Release\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Before a prompt or model goes to production\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full suite, pairwise against production, human spot-check\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Named owner signs off\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Gates that respect noise\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two kinds of metric need two kinds of gate:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Regression cases\u003c/b> (format valid, never mentions competitors, every case that once broke in production) must pass at 100%. Any failure blocks the merge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Capability metrics\u003c/b> (groundedness pass rate, task success) move with noise. Compare with a stored baseline and fail only if the drop exceeds about two standard errors.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># evals/gate.py: exit non-zero if the run regressed\nimport json, math, sys\n\nrun, base = json.load(open(sys.argv[1])), json.load(open(sys.argv[2]))\nMUST_PASS = {\"format_valid\", \"no_forbidden_content\", \"known_regressions\"}\nproblems = []\nfor name, r in run.items():\n    rate = r[\"passed\"] / r[\"n\"]\n    if name in MUST_PASS:\n        if rate &lt; 1.0:\n            problems.append(f\"{name}: {r['n'] - r['passed']} must-pass case(s) failed\")\n        continue\n    b = base[name][\"passed\"] / base[name][\"n\"]\n    se = math.sqrt(rate * (1 - rate) / r[\"n\"] + b * (1 - b) / base[name][\"n\"])\n    if rate &lt; b - 2 * se:\n        problems.append(f\"{name}: {rate:.1%} vs baseline {b:.1%} (allowed drop {2 * se:.1%})\")\nprint(\"\\n\".join(problems) or \"all gates passed\")\nsys.exit(1 if problems else 0)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This unpaired check is deliberately simple; if you store per-case results, the paired comparison from the previous lesson is tighter. Update the baseline only when a change is merged on purpose.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Wiring it into CI\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">yaml\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># .github/workflows/evals.yml\nname: evals\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths: [\"prompts/**\", \"atlas/**\", \"evals/**\"]\njobs:\n  smoke:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 15\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: \"3.12\"\n      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n      - run: python -m evals.run --suite smoke --out results.json\n        env:\n          OPENAI_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.OPENAI_API_KEY }}\n      - run: python evals/gate.py results.json evals/baseline_smoke.json\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly workflow runs \u003ccode>--suite full\u003c/code> on a schedule and posts a trend table (date, commit, prompt version, model, each metric with its interval, cost of the run).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Keeping CI evals trustworthy\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pin model versions\u003c/b> (dated snapshots where the provider offers them), so a silent provider update does not look like your regression.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cache responses\u003c/b> keyed by a hash of model, parameters, prompt version and input, so unchanged cases are not re-run and re-billed on every commit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cap spend\u003c/b> per run and fail loudly if exceeded.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Quarantine flaky cases.\u003c/b> A case that flips between runs with no change is measuring noise; fix it or move it out of the must-pass set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Show the diff.\u003c/b> Post newly failing case ids and their outputs on the pull request. A number alone gets ignored.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Online evaluation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Offline datasets go stale: users ask new things, documents change, providers update models. Online evaluation runs evaluators on production traffic asynchronously, after the response has been sent, and stores the results as scores on traces:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pull request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Smoke evals\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code checks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Merge\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Full evals\u003c/b>\u003csmall>judges, trials\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Deploy\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Online evals\u003c/b>\u003csmall>sampled live traffic\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>New failures\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Dataset\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run cheap \u003cb>code checks on every trace\u003c/b> (format, citations, forbidden content).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003cb>validated judges on a sample\u003c/b>, for example 2 to 5% of traffic, plus every trace with negative feedback.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Alert\u003c/b> when a rate moves beyond its normal noise, and send failing traces to a review queue so they become new dataset cases.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Online evaluators differ from \u003cb>guardrails\u003c/b>: a guardrail sits in the request path and blocks or rewrites a response before the user sees it (Security module); an online evaluator observes afterwards and costs no latency. For large changes, an \u003cb>A/B test\u003c/b> splits real users between versions and compares business outcomes such as resolution rate or escalations. The Observability module shows how to implement online monitoring.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What would you run on every pull request, and what nightly?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On every pull request touching prompts, AI code or evals: a smoke suite of 30 to 50 cases with code checks (and at most a cheap judge), gated on 100% of must-pass regression cases, finishing in a few minutes. Nightly and on any model change: the full suite with validated judges and several trials, gated on capability metrics not dropping more than about two standard errors below the stored baseline, with a trend report.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why gate capability metrics against a baseline with a tolerance rather than a fixed threshold?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Capability scores fluctuate with sampling and output randomness, so a fixed threshold either blocks good changes on noise or is set so low that it misses real drops. A tolerance derived from the standard error flags only drops larger than noise.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does an online evaluator differ from a guardrail?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A guardrail runs synchronously in the request path and can block or change a response, adding latency. An online evaluator scores traces asynchronously after the response is delivered, adds no latency and is used for monitoring, alerting and finding new failures.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:evals/error-analysis-on-50-traces":"\u003cp>You will do the highest-return activity in AI engineering on your own system: read 50 traces, write notes, turn them into a failure taxonomy and count it. Use Atlas (any milestone with retrieval works) or any LLM application you have.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+ and your application importable from Python, or a JSONL export of 50 traces from a tracing tool (Langfuse, Phoenix and LangSmith can all export).\u003c/li>\u003cli>50 inputs: real user queries if you have them, otherwise generate them with the dimension method from the datasets lesson.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One hour without interruptions. Reading is the work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Collect 50 traces with everything needed to judge them: input, retrieved documents, tool calls with results, and output.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># collect.py\nimport json\nfrom atlas import run_with_trace   # returns {\"input\", \"retrieved\", \"tool_calls\", \"output\"}\n\ninputs = [json.loads(line)[\"input\"] for line in open(\"inputs_50.jsonl\")]\nwith open(\"traces.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for i, text in enumerate(inputs):\n        t = run_with_trace(text)\n        t[\"trace_id\"] = f\"t{i:02d}\"\n        f.write(json.dumps(t) + \"\\n\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Build a minimal, resumable annotator. Ten minutes spent here saves the rest of the hour.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># annotate.py\nimport json, os, textwrap\n\ntraces = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"traces.jsonl\")]\ndone = set()\nif os.path.exists(\"notes.jsonl\"):\n    done = {json.loads(line)[\"trace_id\"] for line in open(\"notes.jsonl\")}\n\nwith open(\"notes.jsonl\", \"a\") as out:\n    for t in traces:\n        if t[\"trace_id\"] in done:\n            continue\n        print(\"\\n\" * 3 + f\"== {t['trace_id']} ==\\nUSER: {t['input']}\\n\")\n        for d in t.get(\"retrieved\", []):\n            print(f\"[doc {d['id']}] {d['title']}: {textwrap.shorten(d['text'], 300)}\")\n        for c in t.get(\"tool_calls\", []):\n            print(f\"TOOL {c['name']}({json.dumps(c['args'])}) -&gt; {textwrap.shorten(str(c['result']), 200)}\")\n        print(f\"\\nASSISTANT: {t['output']}\\n\")\n        verdict = input(\"pass or fail [p/f, q to quit]: \").strip().lower()\n        if verdict == \"q\":\n            break\n        note = input(\"first thing that went wrong: \").strip() if verdict == \"f\" else \"\"\n        out.write(json.dumps({\"trace_id\": t[\"trace_id\"], \"pass\": verdict == \"p\", \"note\": note}) + \"\\n\")\n        out.flush()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Open-code all 50 traces. Aim for about 45 seconds each. Write concrete notes about the \u003cb>first\u003c/b> failure (\"cited doc 4, which is about billing, for a security question\"), not categories.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Axial-code with an LLM as an assistant. Paste your failure notes into a strong model with this prompt, then edit its proposal until every category is specific and actionable, and save the result as \u003ccode>categories.json\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Below are notes from reviewing failed traces of a documentation assistant.\nPropose 4 to 8 failure categories. Each must be specific and actionable: something an\nengineer could fix or test. Give each a one-line definition. Then assign every note\nto exactly one category. Return JSON only:\n{\"categories\": [{\"name\": \"...\", \"definition\": \"...\"}],\n \"assignments\": {\"&lt;trace_id&gt;\": \"&lt;category name&gt;\"}}\n\nNotes:\nt03: ...\nt07: ...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Count and rank.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nnotes = [json.loads(line) for line in open(\"notes.jsonl\")]\nassign = json.load(open(\"categories.json\"))[\"assignments\"]\nfails = [n for n in notes if not n[\"pass\"]]\nprint(f\"pass rate: {len(notes) - len(fails)}/{len(notes)}\")\nfor cat, k in Counter(assign[n[\"trace_id\"]] for n in fails).most_common():\n    print(f\"{cat:55s} {k:3d}  {k / len(notes):.0%} of traces\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Decide. For the top three categories write one line each: fix directly (prompt, retrieval, tool description), write a code check, or write an LLM judge. Add every failing trace from the top category to your eval dataset as a regression case.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A pass rate somewhere between 40% and 80% for a young application, and two or three categories covering most failures.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Categories you did not expect before reading. If your taxonomy matches a generic list (\"hallucination, relevance, toxicity\"), your notes were too vague: rewrite a few and recode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>By trace 35 to 50, few new categories appear (approaching saturation).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did you write notes before creating categories?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Categories invented up front bias what you notice and tend to be generic. Writing concrete notes first lets the categories emerge from what actually goes wrong in your system, which is the point of open coding.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your top category is \"retrieval missed the relevant page\". Is an LLM judge the right next step?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Usually not. Retrieval misses are best measured with retrieval metrics such as recall@k on labelled queries (covered in the retrieval modules) and fixed in chunking, indexing or query rewriting. Add the failing queries to the retrieval eval set.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:eval-suite-for-your-rag-or-agent":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M10. You have a provider-agnostic client with cost logging, a prompt registry with a 30 to 50 case regression suite (M3), typed tools, a context assembler, memory, hybrid search, grounded answers with citations and a RAG eval (M8), a graph with human approval for actions (M9) and an MCP server (M10).\u003c/p>\u003cp>This milestone replaces ad hoc checks with an eval suite that is grounded in observed failures and trusted by the team: around 100 cases, a code check for every rule-detectable failure mode, one or two LLM judges validated against your own labels, agent tasks graded on outcomes, and CI that blocks regressions without blocking on noise.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Error analysis on at least 100 traces (at least 50 annotated by you), written up in \u003ccode>evals/ERROR_ANALYSIS.md\u003c/code> with the failure taxonomy and counts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A dataset of at least 100 cases in JSONL with \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>input\u003c/code>, expected fields or grading notes, \u003ccode>tags\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>split\u003c/code>: migrated M3 cases, at least 20 confirmed failures, at least 15 synthetic cases from dimension tuples, at least 10 \"should not\" cases and at least 10 action cases graded on outcome.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Code assertions for every failure mode a rule can detect, each with known-pass and known-fail unit tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One or two LLM judges for the top judgement-based failure modes, validated on at least 100 labelled outputs with an example pool, a dev split and a test split; report TPR, TNR and kappa on the test split.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Action tasks run against a fresh sandbox database per trial, graded on outcome plus trajectory metrics, 3 trials each, reported as pass^3.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One command, \u003ccode>python -m evals.run --suite smoke|full\u003c/code>, writing per-case results, scores with 95% intervals, cost and latency to JSON.\u003c/li>\u003cli>CI: smoke suite on every pull request touching prompts, Atlas code or evals; full suite nightly and on model changes; a gate script with must-pass sets and noise-aware capability gates.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Collect and annotate traces; write the taxonomy and counts. Fix the trivial failures you find straight away.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the dataset: migrate M3 cases, add failures, generate and review synthetic inputs, add negative and action cases, and split dev and test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the code assertions and their unit tests, and run them over the dataset with pytest or promptfoo.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Label outputs for the top judgement-based failure mode, write the judge, iterate on dev, then report test-split TPR, TNR and kappa in \u003ccode>evals/JUDGES.md\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the action tasks with sandbox resets, outcome checks, trajectory metrics and trials.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the runner and gate, record the first baseline, and wire both workflows into CI.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make a deliberate bad change (for example remove the citation instruction), open a pull request and confirm CI blocks it with a readable message.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python -m evals.run --suite full\u003c/code> runs the whole suite from a clean checkout and prints every metric with its interval and the run's cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A pull request that breaks a must-pass case is blocked, and the failing case ids and outputs appear in the CI log.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>evals/JUDGES.md\u003c/code> shows test-split TPR, TNR and kappa for each judge, with the confusion matrix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>evals/TRENDS.md\u003c/code> has at least three rows from separate runs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add a pairwise release check: candidate prompt against production prompt, both orders, win rate with Wilson interval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Report judge-corrected failure rates with the Rogan-Gladen formula.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Port the smoke suite to Inspect and compare two models with \u003ccode>--epochs 3\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cache model responses by request hash to cut CI cost, and report the saving.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Version judge prompts like any other prompt and store every judge critique: they are your best debugging data.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run judges at temperature 0 with JSON output, and fail the run if a verdict does not parse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record the dataset version (a hash of the JSONL file) in every result file, so trends compare like with like.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
